# `emit_abi` writes LF, and `tools/check_abi.js` byte-compares its output against the
# committed file. Under `core.autocrlf=true` git checks that file out as CRLF, so
# `npm run check:abi` fails on a clean freshly-checked-out tree -- and fails opaquely,
# reporting every one of its 221 lines as different while `diff` shows no visible
# change. Pinning the checkout to LF is what makes the generator and the gate agree.
#
# The two states are otherwise mutually exclusive: CRLF gives a clean `git status` and
# a failing `check:abi`, LF gives a passing `check:abi` and a file git reports as
# permanently modified. Neither is a tree anybody should have to work in.
#
# Deliberately scoped to the files that are compared byte for byte, and not
# `* text=auto eol=lf`: that renormalises every file in the working tree at once, which
# is the same enormous unrelated diff AGENTS.md refuses `cargo fmt` for.
#
# **The scoping principle was right and the list was short by two**, which cost
# somebody an afternoon on 2026-08-15. This file said "the one generated file" and
# meant it; but the representative room's sidecar and validator are *also* compared
# byte for byte -- by SHA-256, from `client/src/render/room-asset.generated.ts` -- and
# they are ordinary JSON, so `core.autocrlf=true` rewrote them on checkout and `#/game`
# stopped with `representative room asset failed during sidecar hash`. The sidecar
# holds a single trailing newline: 5,384 bytes became 5,385 and
# `b15c44c4...` became `a693f0d9...`. One byte.
#
# The GLB beside them was never at risk and that is the tell worth remembering -- it
# carries a NUL at offset 5, so git detects it as binary and leaves it alone. The
# failure lands on the *text* member of a set whose other members look fine, which is
# why it reads as a corrupt asset rather than as a checkout setting.
#
# So the rule for adding to this list: if anything compares a file's bytes or hashes
# its contents, it belongs here the day that comparison is written. `binary` on the
# two payloads is belt and braces -- git's own detection already covers them -- but it
# states the intent rather than relying on a heuristic about byte 5.
client/src/protocol/abi.generated.ts text eol=lf
web/assets3d/room_slice.json          text eol=lf
web/assets3d/room_slice.validator.json text eol=lf
web/assets3d/room_slice.glb           binary
web/assets3d/combatants.json          text eol=lf
web/assets3d/combatants.validator.json text eol=lf
web/assets3d/combatants.glb           binary
checkpoints/*.ckpt                    binary

# Vite verifies these generated room records byte-for-byte against SHA-256 values
# in tools/art/manifest.json. A Windows CRLF checkout changes the bytes while Git
# still reports a clean tree, so keep the reviewed artifacts in their generated LF
# form for the same reason as the ABI above.
tools/art/manifest.json text eol=lf
tools/art/combatants-manifest.json text eol=lf
web/assets3d/room_slice.json text eol=lf
web/assets3d/room_slice.validator.json text eol=lf
web/assets3d/combatants.json text eol=lf
web/assets3d/combatants.validator.json text eol=lf

# **The list was short by four a second time, and it cost the same afternoon over.**
# The stanza above states the rule -- if anything hashes a file's contents, it belongs
# here the day that comparison is written -- and then the generator sources that
# `tools/validate_assets.js` hashes into `buildInputsSha256` were left out of it. That
# hash covers `{manifest, generatorSources}`, where the four Python files below are
# read from the working tree; the sidecar's recorded stamp was made from their LF
# bytes. Under `core.autocrlf=true` the checkout hands the validator CRLF instead, so
# every line ending shifts the hash and the gate reports `sidecar build-input hash
# drifted` -- naming the sidecar, which is the one file in the comparison that is
# correct. It reads as a regenerate-the-room problem, and regenerating needs Blender,
# so the false alarm points at the most expensive possible repair.
#
# The tell is the one from the stanza above, inverted: there the *text* member of a
# set failed while its binary neighbours verified. Here the failing inputs are not
# artifacts at all -- they are ordinary source files that a hash happens to read, which
# is why nobody thought of them as reviewed bytes. Any file named in a `sha256(...)`
# call is a reviewed byte whether or not it looks like an asset.
tools/art/build_slice.py text eol=lf
tools/art/export.py      text eol=lf
tools/art/materials.py   text eol=lf
tools/art/room.py        text eol=lf

